        "columns": [
          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
                    "text": "Principles of ecosystemic articulation:",
                    "type": "text"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " when the nodes",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "stop looking to the centre and relate to one another, generating vitality.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Over the last decade, the Social and Solidarity Economy has developed a great capacity for collective organisation throughout Catalonia. Cooperatives, inter-cooperation networks, local solidarity economy networks, federations of cooperatives, territorial hubs, second-tier cooperatives, cooperative ateneus and various forms of alliance have made it possible to consolidate a rooted, collective economy, responding to eco-social needs and building spaces of cooperation that put life at the centre.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "However, as the social and solidarity economy grows and diversifies, a question is becoming increasingly present in many organisations and territories: how can this whole set of initiatives be articulated as an ecosystem?",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This study starts from an observation: building a network is not the same as articulating an ecosystem. Networks make it possible to connect actors, share information, coordinate actions or drive joint projects. But ecosystems work differently: they are living systems of interdependence, where actors do not just cooperate but co-evolve, generate collective capacity and build the conditions for new economic and social possibilities to emerge.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
                    "text": "Authors:",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Laura Comas, Resilience Earth SCCL",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
